{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"Open-source NLP toolboxes like OpenNLP and CoreNLP cover only 6-7 languages.","source_span":"Open-source off-the-shelf natural language processing (NLP) toolboxes like OpenNLP and CoreNLP cover only 6–7 languages"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"Sufficient labeled training data exists for inducing POS models for about 20-30 languages.","source_span":"we have sufficient labeled training data for inducing models for about 20–30 languages"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"The authors' only assumption for low-resource languages is text translated into multiple languages, specifically fragments of the Bible, used for annotation projection and learning cross-lingual word embeddings.","source_span":"Our only assumption is a bit of text translated into multiple languages, specifically, fragments of the Bible."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"The authors use an unsupervised word alignment algorithm to align 15x12 language pairs.","source_span":"we use an unsupervised word alignment algorithm to align $15\\times 12$ language pairs"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"For 15 languages, predicted POS tags exist for each word in the multi-parallel corpus, covering 12 target language training datasets.","source_span":"For 15 languages, we have predicted POS tags for each word in our multi-parallel corpus. For each word in one of our 12 target language training datasets"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"Each word token can have up to 15 votes from source languages, and the paper simply uses the majority vote.","source_span":"we thus have up to 15 votes for each word token, possibly weighted by the confidence of the word alignment algorithm. In this paper, we simply use the majority votes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"Empirical Gaussian priors are applied to linear-chain conditional random fields (CRFs) and averaged structured perceptrons.","source_span":"We will apply empirical Gaussian priors to linear-chain conditional random fields (CRFs; BIBREF3 ) and averaged structured perceptrons BIBREF4 ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"L2-Prior assumes constant variance (sigma^2=C) with mean equal to the average source model parameter, while EmpGauss learns both variance and mean, i.e. empirical Gaussian priors.","source_span":"L2-Prior is the L2-CRF objective with $\\sigma ^2_j=C$ ... and the case where both variances and means are learned – which we call empirical Gaussian priors (EmpGauss)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","statement":"In single-source cross-lingual transfer learning, observed variance is zero, so L2-Prior is equivalent to EmpGauss.","source_span":"in single-source cross-lingual transfer learning, observed variance is zero... In the single-source set-up, L2-Prior is thus equivalent to EmpGauss."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","fact_refs":[0],"question":"About how many different languages do people around the world speak, according to the paper on empirical Gaussian priors for cross-lingual transfer learning?","answer":"About 6,900 different languages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"How many languages do NLP toolboxes like OpenNLP and CoreNLP cover, and how many languages have enough labeled training data for POS models?","answer":"OpenNLP and CoreNLP cover only 6-7 languages, while sufficient labeled training data exists for inducing models for about 20-30 languages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What text resource does the paper rely on for annotation projection and learning cross-lingual word embeddings when no training data or tag dictionaries are available?","answer":"Fragments of the Bible translated into multiple languages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"How many language pairs are aligned by the unsupervised word alignment algorithm, and how many target language training datasets are used?","answer":"The algorithm aligns 15x12 language pairs (15 source languages), and the experiments use 12 target language training datasets."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How does the paper combine multiple source-language POS tag predictions for a given word token?","answer":"Each word token can receive up to 15 votes from source languages, and the paper simply uses the majority vote among them."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0004","fact_refs":[8,9],"question":"What is the difference between the L2-Prior and EmpGauss regularization methods, and when do they become equivalent?","answer":"L2-Prior fixes the variance to a constant C while using the average source model as the mean, whereas EmpGauss learns both the variance and mean from source models. They become equivalent in the single-source cross-lingual transfer setup, since observed variance there is zero."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","statement":"The authors created a new gold standard corpus of 90,000 tokens across 340 documents.","source_span":"We create a new gold standard corpus (90k tokens in 340 documents)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","statement":"Argumentation has been studied since the early work of Aristotle, dating back to the 4th century BC.","source_span":"The art of argumentation has been studied since the early work of Aristotle, dating back to the 4th century BC"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","statement":"Mercier and Sperber (2011) claim that argumentation is what drives humans to perform reasoning.","source_span":"Mercier.Sperber.2011 even claim that argumentation is what drives humans to perform reasoning."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","statement":"Prior argumentation mining approaches have been restricted to specific domains such as hotel reviews, Tweets on local riot events, student essays, airline passenger rights/consumer protection, and renewable energy sources.","source_span":"the scope of the current approaches is restricted to a particular domain or register, e.g., hotel reviews BIBREF5 , Tweets related to local riot events BIBREF6 , student essays BIBREF7 , airline passenger rights and consumer protection BIBREF8 , or renewable energy sources BIBREF9"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","statement":"The authors offer the data, source codes, and annotation guidelines to the community under free licenses.","source_span":"We offer the data, source codes, and annotation guidelines to the community under free licenses."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","statement":"Boltuzic and Snajder (2014) note that user-provided arguments, unlike those in formal debates, are less formal, ambiguous, vague, implicit, or poorly worded.","source_span":"unlike in debates or other more formal argumentation sources, the arguments provided by the users, if any, are less formal, ambiguous, vague, implicit, or often simply poorly worded."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How large is the new gold standard corpus created for this argumentation mining study?","answer":"It contains 90,000 tokens across 340 documents."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Since when has the study of argumentation existed, according to the paper?","answer":"Since the early work of Aristotle, dating back to the 4th century BC."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What do Mercier and Sperber (2011) claim about argumentation's role in human cognition?","answer":"They claim that argumentation is what drives humans to perform reasoning."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What specific domains or registers have prior argumentation mining approaches typically been restricted to?","answer":"Domains such as hotel reviews, Tweets about local riot events, student essays, airline passenger rights and consumer protection, and renewable energy sources."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Under what terms do the authors make their corpus, code, and annotation guidelines available?","answer":"They release the data, source codes, and annotation guidelines to the community under free licenses."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0005","fact_refs":[5],"question":"According to Boltuzic and Snajder (2014), how do user-generated arguments differ from those found in formal debates?","answer":"User-generated arguments, when present, tend to be less formal, ambiguous, vague, implicit, or simply poorly worded compared to more formal argumentation sources."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"The paper proposes a method to evaluate a speech solution's performance without putting it to actual people use.","source_span":"we propose a method to evaluate the performance of a speech solution without actually putting it to people use"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"Typical menu-based speech solutions strive for 3-5 level nodes to remain usable.","source_span":"Typical menu based speech solutions strive for a 3 - 5 level nodes to make it usable."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"In the example banking speech solution, the entry node has a perplexity of 6, with options credit card, savings account, current account, loan product, demat, and mutual fund transfer.","source_span":"the entry node could expect someone to speak among /credit card/, /savings account/, /current account/, /loan product/, /demat/, and /mutual fund transfer/ which has a perplexity of 6"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"After correctly recognizing 'savings account', the second node has perplexity 3, with options account balance, cheque, or last 5 transactions.","source_span":"at the second node it could be /account balance/ or /cheque/ or /last 5 transactions/ (perplexity 3)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"After recognizing 'cheque' at the second node, the third node has perplexity 3, with options new cheque book request, cheque status, and stop cheque request.","source_span":"at the third node (say, on recognition of /cheque/) it could be /new cheque book request/, /cheque status/, and /stop cheque request/ (perplexity 3)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"A transaction in the menu-based speech solution is termed successful if and only if recognition at each of the three nodes is correct.","source_span":"A transaction is termed successful if and only if the recognition at each of the three nodes is correct."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"A recognition error at the entry node is more expensive than a recognition error at a lower node.","source_span":"an error in recognition at the entry node is more expensive than a recognition error at a lower node"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","statement":"The Wizard of Oz (WoZ) approach involves a human agent listening to the conversation and correcting mis-recognitions without the user knowing a human agent is in the loop.","source_span":"this requires a human agent in the loop during actual speech transaction where the human agent corrects any mis-recognition by actually listening to the conversation between the human user and the machine without the user knowing that there is a human agent in the loop"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is the main contribution of this paper about evaluating speech recognition based systems?","answer":"A method to evaluate a speech solution's performance without actually putting it to real people use, saving evaluation time and expense."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","fact_refs":[2],"question":"In the example banking speech solution, what six options are offered at the entry node and what is its perplexity?","answer":"The entry node offers credit card, savings account, current account, loan product, demat, and mutual fund transfer, giving a perplexity of 6."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","fact_refs":[3],"question":"After a user says 'savings account' and it is recognized correctly, what options and perplexity does the second node have?","answer":"The second node offers account balance, cheque, or last 5 transactions, with a perplexity of 3."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What condition must be met for a transaction in a menu-based speech solution to be considered successful?","answer":"Recognition at each of the three nodes in the transaction must be correct."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why is a recognition error at the entry node more costly than one at a lower node?","answer":"Because an error at the entry node is more expensive than a recognition error at a lower node, since it derails the transaction earlier."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0006","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How does the Wizard of Oz (WoZ) approach work when testing a speech solution?","answer":"A human agent listens to the conversation between the user and the machine and corrects any mis-recognition, without the user knowing that a human agent is involved."}
